=== day
n: 2
title: Style it
subtitle: CSS
hours: 3 hours
page: show.html
slot: css | css | style.css
file: style.css = css
refs: @page

--- intro
Yesterday's page works, but it looks plain. Today you give it colours, fonts, spacing and a card design with **CSS**, the language that says how things *look*.

The HTML is the same Friends page you built yesterday (our tidy copy of it). You only write CSS today, in a file called **style.css**. To see which classes and elements you can style, open the **show.html (whole page)** tab above the code box.

At the end you choose your own colours. They'll follow you through the rest of the course.

--- default css
/* style.css: how ShowShelf looks */

--- template
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Friends · ShowShelf</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <a href="index.html" class="logo">ShowShelf</a>
    <nav>
      <a href="index.html">Home</a>
      <a href="about.html">About me</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main>
    <article class="show">
      <h1>Friends</h1>
      <p class="meta">NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode</p>

      <img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">

      <p>
        Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
        break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.
      </p>

      <section>
        <h2>Genres</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Comedy</li>
          <li>Romance</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Main cast</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Jennifer Aniston as Rachel Green</li>
          <li>Courteney Cox as Monica Geller</li>
          <li>Lisa Kudrow as Phoebe Buffay</li>
          <li>Matt LeBlanc as Joey Tribbiani</li>
          <li>Matthew Perry as Chandler Bing</li>
          <li>David Schwimmer as Ross Geller</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Seasons</h2>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Season</th><th>Episodes</th><th>Year</th></tr>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>24</td><td>1994</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>24</td><td>1995</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>25</td><td>1996</td></tr>
        </table>
      </section>

      <p>
        Want more? <a href="https://www.tvmaze.com/shows/431/friends">Read about it on TVmaze</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <p>ShowShelf · Web Dev in 11 Days</p>
  </footer>
</body>
</html>


=== step first-rule
title: Your first CSS rule
minutes: 15
slot: css
--- explain
CSS is written as **rules**. A rule says *which* elements to style, and *how*:

```css
body {
  background-color: lightyellow;
}
```

- `body` is the **selector**: which elements this rule is for. `body` is the whole visible page.
- Inside the curly brackets `{ }` are **declarations**: a **property**, a colon `:`, a **value**, and a semicolon `;` at the end.

How does the page know about style.css? Open the **show.html (whole page)** tab: in the `<head>` there is `<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">`. That line connects the two files.

### Colours
You can write colours as names (`lavender`, `mintcream`, `white`) or as **hex codes**, a `#` and six characters, like `#f6f5fb`. Hex codes give you millions of colours. Search the web for "colour picker" to find the code of any colour you like.
--- task
Give the page a light background colour of your choice, using the `body` selector.

Some ideas: `#f6f5fb` (light violet), `#fdf6e3` (cream), `#eef6f3` (mint), `lavender`.
--- hint
Write the selector `body`, then `{`, then the declaration `background-color: #f6f5fb;` and finally `}`.
--- hint
```css
body {
  background-color: #f6f5fb;
}
```
--- solution
/* style.css: how ShowShelf looks */

body {
  background-color: #f6f5fb;
}
--- check
t.expect(/body\s*\{/.test(t.clean), "Start a rule for the whole page with the selector `body` and curly brackets: `body { ... }`.");
const bg = t.css("body", "background-color");
if (t.isTransparent(bg)) {
  t.expect(!/background-colou?r\s*:[^;{}]*$/m.test(t.clean) || /;/.test(t.clean), "End the declaration with a semicolon `;`.");
  t.expect(!/background-colour/.test(t.clean), "CSS uses American spelling: `background-color` (no u).");
  t.fail("The background hasn't changed yet. Inside `body { }` write `background-color:` then a colour and `;`.");
}
t.expect(!t.isWhite(bg), "That's white, the same as before. Pick a light colour with a little tint, for example `#f6f5fb`.");
t.expect(t.light(bg) > 0.55, "That colour is quite dark: black text on it will be hard to read. Pick a light colour for now. (There's a dark mode challenge later today.)");
--- learned
You wrote a **rule**: a selector, then declarations inside `{ }`. Each declaration is `property: value;`. The browser applied it to every `body` (there's one) straight away.
--- summary
A CSS rule: `selector { property: value; }`.
--- mistakes
- British spelling: `background-colour` doesn't work in CSS.
- Forgetting the `;` or the closing `}`.
- A space inside the hex code: `# f6f5fb`.


=== step text
title: Text colour and font
minutes: 15
slot: css
--- explain
One rule can hold many declarations. Put each on its own line:

```css
body {
  background-color: lightyellow;
  color: #333333;
  font-family: Georgia, serif;
  line-height: 1.5;
}
```

- `color` is the **text** colour. (The background has its own property.)
- `font-family` is a list of fonts, in order of preference. The browser uses the first one the computer has. The last one should be a general family like `sans-serif` or `serif`.
- `line-height` is the space between lines. `1.5` means one and a half times the text size. More space makes long text easier to read.

`system-ui` means "the normal font of this computer", a safe and clean choice.
--- task
In your `body` rule, add:

- a dark text `color` (not pure black: try `#1c1b29`)
- a `font-family` without little "feet", for example `system-ui, Arial, sans-serif`
- a `line-height` of about `1.6`
--- hint
Add three new lines inside the same `body { }` rule, under your background colour. Each ends with `;`.
--- hint
```css
body {
  background-color: #f6f5fb;
  color: #1c1b29;
  font-family: system-ui, Arial, sans-serif;
  line-height: 1.6;
}
```
--- solution-replace
  background-color: #f6f5fb;
}
=>
  background-color: #f6f5fb;
  color: #1c1b29;
  font-family: system-ui, Arial, sans-serif;
  line-height: 1.6;
}
--- check
const ff = t.css("body", "font-family");
t.expect(/font-family\s*:/.test(t.clean), "Add a `font-family` declaration to the body rule.");
t.expect(!/^"?times/i.test(ff) && ff !== "serif", "The font still looks like the default. Try `font-family: system-ui, Arial, sans-serif;` and check the commas and the `;`.");
const lh = t.css("body", "line-height");
t.expect(lh !== "normal", "Add a `line-height`, for example `line-height: 1.6;`.");
t.expect(parseFloat(lh) >= 1.3 * t.px("body", "font-size"), "Make the line height a bit bigger, around `1.5` or `1.6`.");
const c = t.css("body", "color");
t.expect(!t.same(c, "rgb(0, 0, 0)"), "Set a text `color` too. Try a very dark colour that isn't pure black, like `#1c1b29`.");
t.expect(t.light(c) < 0.45, "That text colour is quite light. Use a dark colour so the text stays easy to read.");
t.expect(!t.isTransparent(t.css("body", "background-color")), "Keep your background colour from the last step.");
--- learned
The whole page changed from one rule, because the text elements **inherit** the font and colour of the `body` they're inside. That's the "cascading" in *Cascading Style Sheets*.
--- summary
`color`, `font-family` and `line-height` style text. Elements inherit them from `body`.
--- mistakes
- A second `body { }` rule for each property (works, but keep them together).
- Missing commas between fonts: `system-ui Arial sans-serif`.
- Writing `font: Arial;` instead of `font-family`.


=== step headings
title: Colour the headings
minutes: 10
slot: css
--- explain
A selector can be any element name: `h1`, `p`, `li`, `table`... The rule then styles **every** element with that name.

To give several selectors the same style, separate them with commas:

```css
h1, h2 {
  color: darkgreen;
}
```

This is the same as writing one rule for `h1` and another for `h2`, just shorter.

A new rule goes under the previous one, after its `}`. Leave an empty line between rules to keep things readable.
--- task
Give all `h1` and `h2` headings an accent colour of your choice, in one rule. Try a strong colour, like violet `#5b3fd6`.
--- hint
The selector is `h1, h2`. Inside it, one declaration: `color: ...;`.
--- hint
```css
h1, h2 {
  color: #5b3fd6;
}
```
--- solution-append
h1, h2 {
  color: #5b3fd6;
}
--- check
const h1 = t.css("h1", "color"), h2 = t.css("h2", "color"), body = t.css("body", "color");
t.expect(!t.same(h1, body), "The `h1` still has the same colour as the rest of the text. Add a rule with the selector `h1, h2` and a `color`.");
t.expect(t.same(h1, h2), "The `h2` headings should have the same colour as the `h1`. Use one rule for both: `h1, h2 { ... }` (with a comma).");
t.expect(t.light(h1) < 0.8, "That heading colour is very light and hard to read on a light background. Pick a stronger colour.");
--- learned
An **element selector** (`h1`) styles every element of that kind. A comma lets one rule serve several selectors.
--- summary
Element selectors like `h1` style every element of that kind. Commas group selectors.
--- mistakes
- A space instead of a comma: `h1 h2` means "an h2 inside an h1", which doesn't exist here.


=== step classes
title: Style by class
minutes: 15
slot: css
--- explain
Remember `class="meta"` from yesterday? Now it pays off. To select elements by class, write a **dot** and the class name:

```css
.meta {
  color: grey;
}
```

`.meta` means "every element with `class="meta"`". Only that line changes, not the other paragraphs.

### Sizes
Sizes need a **unit**. The most common:

- `px`: pixels, the dots on the screen. `16px` is normal text size.
- `%`: a percentage of the space available.

`font-size` sets the text size. Always write the unit: `14px`, not `14`.
--- task
Make the facts line (`class="meta"`) smaller and grey: a grey `color` like `#676580` and a `font-size` of `14px`.
--- hint
The selector is `.meta` (with a dot). Inside: `color` and `font-size`.
--- hint
```css
.meta {
  color: #676580;
  font-size: 14px;
}
```
--- solution-append
.meta {
  color: #676580;
  font-size: 14px;
}
--- check
t.expect(/\.meta\s*\{/.test(t.clean) || !/\bmeta\s*\{/.test(t.clean), "A class selector starts with a dot: `.meta`, not `meta`.");
const meta = t.$(".meta"), p = t.$$("article p").find((x) => !x.classList.contains("meta"));
t.expect(t.px(meta, "font-size") < t.px(p, "font-size"), "The facts line should be smaller than the other text. Add `font-size: 14px;` to `.meta` (don't forget `px`).");
t.expect(!t.same(t.css(meta, "color"), t.css("body", "color")), "Give the facts line a grey `color`, different from the normal text.");
t.expect(t.same(t.css(p, "color"), t.css("body", "color")), "Only the facts line should turn grey. Use the class selector `.meta`, not `p`.");
--- learned
A **class selector** (`.meta`) styles only the elements with that label. This is how you'll style almost everything from now on: give it a class in HTML, style the class in CSS.
--- summary
`.name` selects elements with `class="name"`. Sizes need units like `px`.
--- mistakes
- No dot: `meta { }` looks for a `<meta>` element.
- A number without a unit: `font-size: 14;` is ignored.
- A dot in the HTML: `class=".meta"`.


=== step card
title: The box model: make a card
minutes: 20
slot: css
--- explain
To the browser, every element is a **box**. Each box has four layers, from inside to outside:

```
┌──────────────── margin ────────────────┐
│  ┌──────────── border ──────────────┐  │
│  │  ┌───────── padding ──────────┐  │  │
│  │  │         content            │  │  │
│  │  └────────────────────────────┘  │  │
│  └──────────────────────────────────┘  │
└────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

- **content**: the text or picture
- **padding**: space *inside* the border, around the content
- **border**: a line around the box
- **margin**: space *outside* the border, between this box and its neighbours

```css
.box {
  background-color: white;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 2px solid black;
  border-radius: 10px;
}
```

`border` takes three values: thickness, style (`solid`, `dashed`, `dotted`) and colour. `border-radius` rounds the corners.
--- task
The whole show is inside `<article class="show">`. Turn it into a white card:

- `background-color: #ffffff;`
- `padding` of `24px`
- a thin `border`, like `1px solid #e3e0f0`
- rounded corners with `border-radius: 12px;`
--- hint
The selector is `.show`. Put the four declarations inside it.
--- hint
```css
.show {
  background-color: #ffffff;
  padding: 24px;
  border: 1px solid #e3e0f0;
  border-radius: 12px;
}
```
--- solution-append
.show {
  background-color: #ffffff;
  padding: 24px;
  border: 1px solid #e3e0f0;
  border-radius: 12px;
}
--- check
const s = t.$(".show");
t.expect(/\.show\s*\{/.test(t.clean), "Start a rule with the selector `.show` (with a dot).");
t.expect(!t.isTransparent(t.css(s, "background-color")), "Give the card a `background-color`, for example `#ffffff`.");
t.expect(t.px(s, "padding-top") >= 8 && t.px(s, "padding-left") >= 8, "Add some `padding` inside the card, like `padding: 24px;`.");
t.expect(t.css(s, "border-top-style") !== "none" && t.px(s, "border-top-width") >= 1, "Add a `border`: thickness, style and colour, like `border: 1px solid #e3e0f0;`.");
t.expect(t.px(s, "border-top-left-radius") >= 4, "Round the corners with `border-radius`, like `border-radius: 12px;`.");
--- learned
You used three layers of the **box model**: padding inside, the border around it, and the background under both. Open the preview's **Bigger** view to see the card at full size.
--- summary
Box model: content, padding (inside), border, margin (outside).
--- mistakes
- `border: 1px #e3e0f0;` without a style (`solid`): no border appears.
- Mixing up padding and margin.


=== step centre
title: Width and centring
minutes: 15
slot: css
--- explain
On a wide screen, very long lines of text are tiring to read. `max-width` stops a box from getting wider than a size you choose:

```css
.box {
  max-width: 600px;
}
```

On a phone the box is still allowed to be narrower. That's why it's `max-width` and not `width`.

To **centre** a box, give it the same margin on the left and on the right. The value `auto` asks the browser to share the free space equally:

```css
.box {
  margin: 40px auto;
}
```

With two values, the first one is for top and bottom, the second for left and right.

> The preview is narrow. Press **Bigger** above it to see the centring on a big screen.
--- task
In your `.show` rule, add a `max-width` of `640px` and a `margin` of `32px auto`.
--- hint
Add two lines inside your existing `.show { }` rule: `max-width: 640px;` and `margin: 32px auto;`.
--- hint
```css
.show {
  max-width: 640px;
  margin: 32px auto;
  background-color: #ffffff;
  padding: 24px;
  border: 1px solid #e3e0f0;
  border-radius: 12px;
}
```
--- solution-replace
.show {
  background-color: #ffffff;
=>
.show {
  max-width: 640px;
  margin: 32px auto;
  background-color: #ffffff;
--- check
const s = t.$(".show");
const mw = t.css(s, "max-width");
t.expect(mw !== "none", "Add `max-width: 640px;` to the `.show` rule.");
t.expect(parseFloat(mw) >= 300 && parseFloat(mw) <= 1000, `A max-width of ${mw} is unusual. Try \`640px\`.`);
const rules = t.rules((sel) => /\.show\b/.test(sel));
t.expect(rules.some((r) => r.style.marginLeft === "auto" && r.style.marginRight === "auto"), "To centre the card, the left and right margins must be `auto`: `margin: 32px auto;`.");
t.expect(t.px(s, "margin-top") >= 8, "Give the card some space above and below: the first margin value, like `32px`.");
--- learned
`max-width` keeps text readable on big screens, and `margin: ... auto` centres a box. You'll use this pair on almost every website.
--- summary
`max-width` limits the width; `margin: 0 auto` centres a box.
--- mistakes
- `width: 640px` instead of `max-width`: the card is too wide for phones.
- `margin: auto 32px` (values swapped): no space above, and it isn't centred.


=== step poster
title: Size the poster
minutes: 10
slot: css
--- explain
Images can be sized with CSS too. If you set only the `width`, the browser works out the height so the picture keeps its shape.

```css
.photo {
  width: 150px;
  border-radius: 50%;
}
```

(`50%` rounding on a square picture makes a circle.)
--- task
Style the poster (`class="poster"`): a `width` of `200px` and slightly rounded corners, `border-radius: 8px`.
--- hint
The selector is `.poster`.
--- hint
```css
.poster {
  width: 200px;
  border-radius: 8px;
}
```
--- solution-append
.poster {
  width: 200px;
  border-radius: 8px;
}
--- check
const img = t.$(".poster");
const w = img.getBoundingClientRect().width;
t.expect(w >= 100 && w <= 320, `The poster is ${Math.round(w)}px wide. Set \`width: 200px;\` in a \`.poster\` rule.`);
t.expect(t.px(img, "border-top-left-radius") >= 3, "Round the poster's corners a little: `border-radius: 8px;`.");
--- learned
Set one side of an image and the browser keeps its shape. Classes let you style this one picture without touching any others.
--- summary
Size images with `width`; the height follows.


=== step header
title: Header and footer
minutes: 20
slot: css
--- explain
Now style the top bar and the footer. Look at the whole page tab: the header has `class="site-header"`, the logo link has `class="logo"`, and the footer has `class="site-footer"`.

Useful new properties:

- `border-bottom`: a line only at the bottom (there's also `border-top`, `border-left`, `border-right`)
- `font-weight: bold;` makes text bold
- `text-decoration: none;` removes the underline from a link
- `text-align: center;` centres text inside its box
--- task
1. `.site-header`: a white `background-color`, `padding: 16px 24px;` and a `border-bottom` like `1px solid #e3e0f0`
2. `.logo`: `font-weight: bold;`, a bigger `font-size` (like `22px`) and `text-decoration: none;`
3. `.site-footer`: `text-align: center;` and a grey `color`
--- hint
Three rules, one per class: `.site-header { }`, `.logo { }` and `.site-footer { }`.
--- hint
```css
.site-header {
  background-color: #ffffff;
  padding: 16px 24px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #e3e0f0;
}

.logo {
  font-weight: bold;
  font-size: 22px;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.site-footer {
  text-align: center;
  color: #676580;
}
```
--- solution-append
.site-header {
  background-color: #ffffff;
  padding: 16px 24px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #e3e0f0;
}

.logo {
  font-weight: bold;
  font-size: 22px;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.site-footer {
  text-align: center;
  color: #676580;
}
--- check
const h = t.$(".site-header");
t.expect(!t.isTransparent(t.css(h, "background-color")), "Give `.site-header` a `background-color`.");
t.expect(t.px(h, "padding-top") >= 8, "Add `padding` to `.site-header`, like `padding: 16px 24px;`.");
t.expect(t.css(h, "border-bottom-style") !== "none", "Add a `border-bottom` to `.site-header`, like `1px solid #e3e0f0`.");
const logo = t.$(".logo");
t.expect(parseInt(t.css(logo, "font-weight")) >= 600, "Make the logo bold: `font-weight: bold;` in a `.logo` rule.");
t.expect(!/underline/.test(t.css(logo, "text-decoration-line")), "Remove the logo's underline: `text-decoration: none;`.");
t.expect(t.px(logo, "font-size") > t.px("body", "font-size"), "Make the logo text bigger with `font-size`, like `22px`.");
t.expect(t.css(".site-footer", "text-align") === "center", "Centre the footer text: `.site-footer { text-align: center; }`.");
--- learned
`border-bottom`, `font-weight`, `text-decoration` and `text-align` are small properties with a big effect. The page now looks like a real website.
--- summary
Style parts of the page by their class: header, logo, footer.


=== step links
title: Links and hover
minutes: 15
slot: css
--- explain
Links are blue and underlined by default. You can change that, and also say how a link looks **while the mouse is over it**, with `:hover` after the selector:

```css
a {
  color: darkgreen;
}

a:hover {
  color: black;
}
```

`:hover` is called a **pseudo-class**: a state the element can be in. Move your mouse over the links in the preview to try it.
--- task
1. Give all links (`a`) your accent colour, the same as your headings.
2. Add an `a:hover` rule that changes the colour to a darker shade, like `#3d2a99`.
--- hint
Two rules: `a { color: ...; }` and `a:hover { color: ...; }`. There's no space between `a` and `:hover`.
--- hint
```css
a {
  color: #5b3fd6;
}

a:hover {
  color: #3d2a99;
}
```
--- solution-append
a {
  color: #5b3fd6;
}

a:hover {
  color: #3d2a99;
}
--- check
const a = t.$("article a");
t.expect(!t.same(t.css(a, "color"), "rgb(0, 0, 238)"), "The links are still the default blue. Add a rule for `a` with your accent `color`.");
const hov = t.rules((sel) => /(^|[\s,])a:hover/.test(sel));
t.expect(hov.length, "Add a second rule with the selector `a:hover` (no space before the colon).");
t.expect(hov.some((r) => r.style.color || r.style.textDecoration || r.style.backgroundColor), "Inside `a:hover { }`, change the `color`.");
--- learned
`:hover` styles an element while the mouse is over it. It's the first bit of interaction on your page, and it needed no programming.
--- summary
`a:hover` styles a link while the mouse is over it.
--- mistakes
- A space: `a :hover` means something different and doesn't work.


=== step table
title: A tidy table
minutes: 15
slot: css
--- explain
Tables look cramped by default. Two tricks make them clean:

- `border-collapse: collapse;` on the `table` merges the double lines between cells into one.
- `padding` on the cells (`th, td`) gives the numbers room to breathe.

```css
table {
  border-collapse: collapse;
}

th, td {
  padding: 6px 16px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #dddddd;
  text-align: left;
}
```
--- task
Style the seasons table: `border-collapse: collapse;` on `table`, and on `th, td` some `padding`, a `border-bottom` and `text-align: left;`.
--- hint
Two rules: `table { }` and `th, td { }`.
--- hint
```css
table {
  border-collapse: collapse;
}

th, td {
  padding: 6px 16px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #e3e0f0;
  text-align: left;
}
```
--- solution-append
table {
  border-collapse: collapse;
}

th, td {
  padding: 6px 16px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #e3e0f0;
  text-align: left;
}
--- check
t.expect(t.css("table", "border-collapse") === "collapse", "Add `border-collapse: collapse;` to a `table` rule.");
const td = t.$("td");
t.expect(t.px(td, "padding-left") >= 6, "Give the cells some `padding` in a `th, td` rule, like `padding: 6px 16px;`.");
t.expect(t.css(td, "border-bottom-style") !== "none", "Add a `border-bottom` to the cells, like `1px solid #e3e0f0`.");
t.expect(t.css("th", "text-align") === "left" || t.css("th", "text-align") === "start", "Line the headings up on the left: `text-align: left;` in the `th, td` rule.");
--- learned
Collapsed borders and padding turn a cramped grid into a readable table.
--- summary
`border-collapse: collapse` and cell padding make tables readable.


=== step theme
title: Your own colours with variables
minutes: 25
slot: css
provides: theme
--- explain
Your accent colour now appears in several rules: headings and links. To change it, you'd have to find and edit every copy. CSS **variables** fix that: define a colour once, give it a name, and use the name everywhere.

```css
:root {
  --color-accent: #0a7d5a;
}

h1 {
  color: var(--color-accent);
}
```

- `:root` means "the whole document". Variables defined there work everywhere.
- A variable name starts with two dashes: `--color-accent`.
- `var(--color-accent)` uses it.

Now changing one line changes the colour everywhere.

> **This is your ShowShelf colour scheme.** From tomorrow on, the Lab uses your `--color-accent` and `--color-bg` in every page you build. Pick colours you like.
--- task
1. At the top of your CSS, add a `:root` rule with two variables: `--color-accent` (your favourite strong colour) and `--color-bg` (a light background colour).
2. Use `var(--color-accent)` for the headings (`h1, h2`) and the links (`a`).
3. Use `var(--color-bg)` for the body's background.

Then change `--color-accent` to a different colour and watch the headings and links change together.
--- hint
The `:root { }` rule holds the two variables. Then, in your existing rules, replace the colour codes with `var(--color-accent)` or `var(--color-bg)`.
--- hint
```css
:root {
  --color-accent: #5b3fd6;
  --color-bg: #f6f5fb;
}

body {
  background-color: var(--color-bg);
  ...
}

h1, h2 {
  color: var(--color-accent);
}
```
And in the `a` rule: `color: var(--color-accent);`
--- solution-replace
body {
  background-color: #f6f5fb;
=>
:root {
  --color-accent: #5b3fd6;
  --color-bg: #f6f5fb;
}

body {
  background-color: var(--color-bg);
--- solution-replace
h1, h2 {
  color: #5b3fd6;
}
=>
h1, h2 {
  color: var(--color-accent);
}
--- solution-replace
a {
  color: #5b3fd6;
}
=>
a {
  color: var(--color-accent);
}
--- check
const accent = t.cssVar("--color-accent"), bgv = t.cssVar("--color-bg");
t.expect(/:root\s*\{/.test(t.clean), "Start with a `:root { }` rule at the top, with a colon before `root`.");
t.expect(accent, "Define `--color-accent` inside `:root`, with two dashes: `--color-accent: #5b3fd6;`.");
t.expect(bgv, "Define `--color-bg` inside `:root` too.");
const probe = t.doc.createElement("i");
t.doc.body.appendChild(probe);
probe.style.color = "var(--color-accent)";
const accentRgb = t.win.getComputedStyle(probe).color;
probe.style.color = "var(--color-bg)";
const bgRgb = t.win.getComputedStyle(probe).color;
probe.remove();
t.expect(/var\(\s*--color-accent\s*\)/.test(t.clean), "Use the variable in your rules: `color: var(--color-accent);`.");
t.expect(t.same(t.css("h1", "color"), accentRgb), "The `h1, h2` rule should use `color: var(--color-accent);`.");
t.expect(t.same(t.css("article a", "color"), accentRgb), "The `a` rule should use `color: var(--color-accent);` too.");
t.expect(t.same(t.css("body", "background-color"), bgRgb), "The body's `background-color` should be `var(--color-bg)`.");
t.expect(t.light(bgRgb) > 0.55, "Keep `--color-bg` light, so the text stays easy to read.");
--- learned
Variables let you define a value once and reuse it everywhere. Your `--color-accent` and `--color-bg` are now **your** ShowShelf colours: you'll see them again tomorrow.
--- summary
CSS variables: define `--name: value;` in `:root`, use it with `var(--name)`.
--- mistakes
- One dash instead of two: `-color-accent`.
- `var(color-accent)` without the dashes.
- `root { }` without the colon.
--- teacher
The student's `:root` block is carried to Days 3 onwards automatically. If a student picks a dark `--color-bg`, later pages will be hard to read: suggest a light one.


=== step dark
title: Dark mode
minutes: 20
slot: css
bonus: yes
--- explain
Many people set their computer to **dark mode**. Websites can follow that setting with a **media query**: CSS that only applies when a condition is true.

```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    --color-bg: #12111a;
  }
}
```

Because your colours are variables, you only need to give them new values inside the media query. That's the real power of variables.

To see it, switch your computer to dark mode (on Windows: Settings, Personalisation, Colours; on a Mac: System Settings, Appearance).
--- task
Add a `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` block that sets new, dark values for `--color-bg`, plus a light text colour. Tip: add a `--color-text` variable and use it in `body` for this.
--- hint
Inside the media query goes a `:root { }` rule with the dark values. Two pairs of curly brackets: one for `@media`, one for `:root`.
--- hint
```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    --color-bg: #12111a;
    --color-text: #f1f0f7;
    --color-accent: #a18bff;
  }
}
```
And in `body`: `color: var(--color-text);` (define `--color-text: #1c1b29;` in your normal `:root`).
--- solution-replace
  --color-bg: #f6f5fb;
}
=>
  --color-bg: #f6f5fb;
  --color-text: #1c1b29;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    --color-bg: #12111a;
    --color-text: #f1f0f7;
    --color-accent: #a18bff;
  }
}
--- solution-replace
  color: #1c1b29;
=>
  color: var(--color-text);
--- check
t.expect(/@media\s*\(\s*prefers-color-scheme\s*:\s*dark\s*\)\s*\{/.test(t.clean), "Start a block with `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {`.");
const m = t.clean.match(/@media\s*\(\s*prefers-color-scheme\s*:\s*dark\s*\)\s*\{([\s\S]*)/);
t.expect(/:root\s*\{[^}]*--color-bg\s*:/.test(m[1]), "Inside the media query, put a `:root { }` rule that sets `--color-bg` to a dark colour.");
let ok = false;
for (const sh of t.doc.styleSheets) for (const r of sh.cssRules) if (r.media && /dark/.test(r.media.mediaText)) ok = true;
t.expect(ok, "The browser couldn't read your media query. Check that every `{` has its `}`.");
--- learned
A **media query** applies CSS only when a condition is true. With variables, a whole dark theme is just a few new values.
--- summary
`@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` for a dark theme.


=== step hover-poster
title: A poster that moves
minutes: 15
slot: css
bonus: yes
--- explain
`transform` can move, rotate or resize an element without changing the layout around it. `transition` makes the change smooth instead of instant:

```css
.photo {
  transition: transform 0.2s;
}

.photo:hover {
  transform: scale(1.05) rotate(-2deg);
}
```

`scale(1.05)` means 5% bigger. `0.2s` is two tenths of a second.
--- task
Make the poster grow a little and tilt when the mouse is over it, smoothly.
--- hint
Add `transition: transform 0.2s;` to `.poster`, and a new `.poster:hover` rule with a `transform`.
--- hint
```css
.poster:hover {
  transform: scale(1.05) rotate(-2deg);
}
```
--- solution-replace
.poster {
  width: 200px;
  border-radius: 8px;
}
=>
.poster {
  width: 200px;
  border-radius: 8px;
  transition: transform 0.2s;
}

.poster:hover {
  transform: scale(1.05) rotate(-2deg);
}
--- check
const hov = t.rules((sel) => /\.poster:hover/.test(sel));
t.expect(hov.length && hov.some((r) => r.style.transform), "Add a `.poster:hover` rule with a `transform`, like `transform: scale(1.05);`.");
t.expect(/transition/.test(t.css(".poster", "transition-property")) || t.css(".poster", "transition-duration") !== "0s", "Add a `transition` to `.poster` so the change is smooth: `transition: transform 0.2s;`.");
--- learned
`transform` plus `transition` gives smooth little animations, all in CSS.
--- summary
`transform` and `transition` for small animations.


=== step caps
title: Small, spaced headings
minutes: 10
slot: css
bonus: yes
--- explain
Two more text properties designers love:

- `text-transform: uppercase;` shows the text in capitals, without changing the HTML.
- `letter-spacing: 1px;` adds space between the letters.

Together they make neat, quiet subheadings.
--- task
Make the `h2` headings uppercase, a bit smaller (`16px`) and spaced out (`letter-spacing: 1px`). Keep the `h1` as it is.
--- hint
Add a new rule for `h2` alone, under the `h1, h2` rule.
--- hint
```css
h2 {
  font-size: 16px;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 1px;
}
```
--- solution-append
h2 {
  font-size: 16px;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 1px;
}
--- check
t.expect(t.css("h2", "text-transform") === "uppercase", "Add `text-transform: uppercase;` to an `h2` rule.");
t.expect(t.px("h2", "letter-spacing") > 0, "Add some `letter-spacing` to the `h2`, like `1px`.");
t.expect(t.css("h1", "text-transform") !== "uppercase", "Only the `h2` should be uppercase: put it in a rule for `h2` alone.");
--- learned
When two rules style the same element, both apply. For the same property, the later rule wins.
--- summary
`text-transform` and `letter-spacing` style headings.
